{
  "name": "Opening Up Space: What to Consider for Walker Accessible Doorways",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Making your doorways friendly for a walker user involves more than just thinking about width, although that's super important too! When you're assessing a doorway, first, measure the usable opening to make sure there's enough room not only for the walker but also for the person's shoulders and hands as they navigate through. Typically, 32-36 inches of clear clearance is a good range for comfortable navigation. But don't stop there! You also need to inspect thresholds; even a slight bump can be a tripping hazard or make pushing a walker difficult. Door hardware matters too – is it easy to grasp and operate with one hand? Any tight turns in the hallway leading up to the door? These can all make a seemingly accessible doorway a challenge. HH Remodeling LLC, based in Aurora and serving places like Centennial, Parker, and Colorado Springs, helps folks address these details. We can take a look at your specific situation and recommend the least disruptive modifications to make your home easier to navigate. If you've got a doorway that's just not working, give us a ring at 303-505-0866 for some personalized advice.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
